","To find the amount of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Phos-Fluor Rinse, we first need to understand the concentration unit (0.044% w/v).

0.044% (w/v) means 0.044 grams of sodium fluoride per 100 mL of solution.

Now, we can set up a proportion to find the amount of sodium fluoride in 10 mL of solution:

\[
\frac{0.044 \text{ g NaF}}{100 \text{ mL solution}} = \frac{x \text{ g NaF}}{10 \text{ mL solution}}
\]

Solving for x:

\[
x = 10 \times \frac{0.044}{100} = 0.0044 \text{ g NaF}
\]

Now, we need to convert grams to milligrams. Since 1 g = 1000 mg, we have:

\[
0.0044 \text{ g NaF} \times 1000 \text{ mg/g} = 4.4 \text{ mg NaF}
\]

So, there are 4.4 milligrams of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Phos-Fluor Rinse.",fluency,"# Task Description
