2. **Coordination Geometry**: The iron ion in the heme group of hemoglobin has a six-coordinate coordination geometry. This means it is bonded to six atoms or ligands. The coordination geometry is approximately octahedral, but it is distorted due to the presence of the different ligands and the structural environment of the protein.

