","To solve this problem, we use the relative speeds of the jugjer and the train.

The speed of the jugjer is 9 km/hr, and the speed of the train is 45 km/hr. Since both are moving in the same direction, the relative speed of the train with respect to the jugjer is:
\[ \text{Relative speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} - 9 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \text{ km/hr} \]
Convert the relative speed to meters per second:
\[ 36 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 36 \times \frac{10}{36} \text{ m/s} = 10 \text{ m/s} \]
The initial distance between the jogjer and the end of the train is 150 m. The time taken for the train to pass the jugjer is 25 seconds. During this time, the distance covered by the tray relative to the jugjer is the sum of the initial distance between the tray and the jugjer (150 m) and the length of the tray (L). Since the tray is moving at 10 m/s, the distance covered in 25 seconds is:
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 250 \text{ m} \]
The distance covered by the tray relative to the jugjer is also equal to the sum of the initial distance between the tray and the jugjer and the length of the tray:
\[ 250 = 150 + L \]
Solving for L:
\[ L = 250 - 150 = 100 \text{ m} \]
Thus, the length of the tray is 100 meters.

The tray must travel the initial distance (150 m) and its length (L) to completely overcome the jugjere. The relative speed is 10 m/s, and the time taken is 25 seconds.
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} \]
\[ 150 + L = 10 \times 25 \]
\[ 150 + L = 250 \]
\[ L = 250 - 150 = 100 \text{ m} \]
The length of the tray is 100 meters.

