The decay length \(l_{\text{eff}}\) of an evanescent wave is inversely proportional to the difference between the effective refractive index \(n_{\text{eff}}\) and the refractive index of the surrounding medium \(n_f\). The formula for the decay length is:

\[
l_{\text{eff}} = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi |n_{\text{eff}} - n_f|}
\]

Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu\text{m} = 1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\)

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
l_{\text{eff}} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}}{2\pi \times 0.3} \approx \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{1.885} \, \text{m} \approx 0.53 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m} = 530 \, \text{nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{530 \, \text{nm}}\).
