An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we first need to find the original and new closed-loop voltages. The formula for the voltage of a negative-freeback amplifier is:

\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + A_v m_v}
\]

Given:
- Initial voltage gain: \( A_v = 100 \)
- Fraction of feedbacks: \( m_v = 0.1 \)

First, calculate the initial voltage with the voltage:
\[
A_{vf,1} = \frac{100}{1 + 100 \times 0.1} = \frac{100}{1 + 10} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \, \text{V}
\]

The voltage is reduced by 6 dB due to aging. The new voltage \( A_{v2} \) is:
\[
A_{v2} = A_v \times 10^{-6/20} = 100 \times 10^{-0.3} \approx 100 \times 0.501 = 50.1 \, \text{V}
\]

Now, calculate the new voltage with the new voltage:
\[
A_{vf,2} = \frac{50.1}{1 + 50.1 \times 0.1} = \frac{50.1}{1 + 5.01} = \frac{50.1}{6.01} \approx 8.33 \, \text{V}
\]

The percentage change in the voltage is:
\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf,2} - A_{vf,1}}{A_{vf,1}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{8.33 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-0.76}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -8.36\%
\]

Thus, the percentage change in the voltage is approximately -8.36%.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

