","To calculate the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the conservation of momentum. The total momentum of the system (ball + Earth) must remain conserved. The system is initially at rest, so the initial momentum is zero. After the cannonball is fired, the system has momentum equal to the momentum of the cannonball. The momentum of the cannonball is equal to its mass times its velocity. The recoil velocity of the Earth is the velocity it gains due to the recoil.

The conservation of momentum equation is:

\[
m_{\text{ball}} v_{\text{ball}} = - m_{\text{Earth}} v_{\text{Earth}}
\]

Here,
- \(m_{\text{block}}\) is the mass of the cannonball = 1.0 kg
- \(v_{\text{block}}\) is the velocity of the cannonblock = 90.0 m/s
- \(m_{\text{Earth}}\) is the mass of the Earth = \(5.97 \times 10^{24}\) kg
- \(v_{\text{Earth}}\) is the velocity of the Earth (recoil velocity)

Rearranging the equation to solve for \(v_{\text{Earth}}\):

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{m_{\text{ball}} v_{\text{Earth}}}{m_{\text{Earth}}}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} = -\frac{(1.0 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}}
\]

\[
v_{\textEarth}} = -\frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s}
\]

\[
v_{\text{Earth}} \approx -1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the opposite direction of the cannonball. The magnitude of the velocity is \(1.51 \times 10^{-23}\) m/s.

The approximate recoil velocity of the Earth is \(1.51 \times 10^{-23}\) m/s.",relevance,"# Task Description
