<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead-acid (lead-sundance) series cell is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \]  
This reaction occurs at the negative electrode (anode).  

Simultaneous with this, at the positive electrode (cathode), the reaction is:  
\[ \text{PbO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

The overall or net reaction is the sum of these two half-reactions:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

Thus, the net reaction is:  
\[ \text{\[
\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}
\]\]  
During discharge, lead and lead dioxide are converted to lead sulfate, and sulfate ions in the electrolyte are consumed, forming water.  
