","To determine the total entropy change of the combined system, we need to consider the entropy changes of both the gas and the water. The entropy change of the gas is due to its cooling process, and the entropy change of the water is due to the heat absorbed during vaporization.

1. Entropy change of the gas:
The gas cools from 1100°C to 550°C. The entropy change of the gas is given by:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{gas}} = \int_{T_1}^{T_2} \frac{c_p}{T} \, dT = c_p \ln \left( \frac{T_2}{T_1} \right)
\]
Here, $c_p = 1.005 \, \text{kJ/kg K}$. The temperatures are in Celsius, so we need to convert them to Kelvin:
\[
T_1 = 1100 + 273 = 1373 \, \text{K}
\]
\[
T_2 = 550 + 273 = 823 \, \text{K}
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{gas}} = 1.005 \times 10^3 \times \ln \left( \frac{823}{1373} \right) = 1005 \ln \left( \frac{823}{1373} \right) \approx 1005 \times (-0.718) \approx -721 \, \text{J/K}
\]

2. Entropy change of the water:
The latent heat of vaporization of water at 220°C is given as 1858.5 kJ/kg. The entropy change of the water during vaporization is:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{water}} = \frac{L}{T_{\text{vap}}}
\]
Here, $L = 1858.5 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/kg}$ and $T_{\text{vap}} = 220 + 273 = 493 \, \text{K}$.
\[
\Delta S_{\text{water}} = \frac{1858.5 \times 10^3}{493} \approx 3774 \, \text{J/K}
\]

3. Total entropy change:
The total entropy change of the combined system is the sum of the entropy changes of the gas and the water:
\[
\Delta S_{\text{total}} = \Delta S_{\text{gas}} + \Delta S_{\text{water}} = -721 + 3774 = 3053 \, \text{J/K}
\]

