The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{i} P(x, y) + \mathbf{J} Q(x, y)\), where \(P\) and \(Q\) are scalar functions of \(x\) and \(y\), is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial R}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Here, \(\mathbf{V} = P(x, y) \mathbf{i} + Q(x, y) \mathbf{j} + 0 \mathbf{k}\), so \(P = P(x, y)\), \(Q = Q(x, y)\), and \(R = 0\). Substituting into the formula for the curl:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Since the vector field depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the components \(z\) are constant, so \(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0\) and \(\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} = 0\). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\mathbf{V} = \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:
