A ball moving on a smooth horizontal table hits a rough vertical wall. The coefficient of restitution between the ball and the wall is \( \frac{1}{3} \). The ball rebounds at the same angle as its angle of incidence. What fraction of its kinetic energy is lost during the collision?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The coefficient of restitution \( e \) is given by:
\[
e = \frac{v_2}{v_1}
\]
where \( v_1 \) is the velocity of the block along the normal to the wall (velocity of approach) and \( v_2 \) is the velocity of the block after the collision (velocity of separation) along the normal to the body.

Given \( e = \frac{1}{3} \), we have:
\[
v_2 = e v_1 = \frac{1}{3} v_1
\]
The velocity of the ball at the time of collision is \( v \). The component of the velocity perpendicular to the wall is \( v \cos \theta \), and the component parallel to the axis is \( v \sin \theta \), where \( \theta \) is the angle of incidence.

The velocity of approach perpendicular to the wall is \( v \cos \theta \), and the velocity of separation perpendicular to the wall is \( -e v \cos \theta = -\frac{1}{3} v \cos \theta \).
The component of velocity parallel to the axis remains unchanged, so it is still \( v \sin \theta \).

