<GROUND_TRUTH>
The curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in Cartesian coordinates is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \hat{e}_z.
\]
Here, \(\frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\) and \(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y}\) are the partial derivatives of the \(y\)- and \(x\)-components of \(\mathbf{V}\), respectively. The result is a vector pointing in the \(z\)-direction because the curl operation in 2D (with \(\mathbf{V}\) independent of \(z\)) simplifies to this form. 

